1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
A friend calls you late at night, wrecked over a decision.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,000
In ten minutes you say the right thing. Calm, clear, kind.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,000
You always know what other people should do.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Then your turn comes, and you freeze. Weeks of circling,

5
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
deciding nothing. You decide you are a hypocrite.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
But you are not a hypocrite. You are running a glitch researchers call Solomon's paradox,

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
named for the king who was wise for everyone but himself.

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
By the end you will know why your mind turns sharp for other people and blunt for you,

9
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
where that split came from, and the one shift in how you talk to

10
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,000
yourself that hands your own advice back.

11
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The name comes from an old story. Solomon was the king

12
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,000
people traveled for miles to consult,

13
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,000
the one who could untangle any quarrel dragged into his court.

14
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Two women, one baby,

15
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
a single clever sentence, and the real mother

16
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,000
revealed herself. People called him the wisest man alive.

17
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Yet in his own house he was a disaster. He married hundreds of times,

18
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,000
taxed his people until they seethed,

19
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
and left a kingdom so brittle it cracked in two the moment he died.

20
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Brilliant for the whole world. Foolish at home.

21
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Three thousand years later, two psychologists decided to find out whether the same

22
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
split is sitting inside ordinary people like you.

23
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Igor Grossmann and Ethan Kross built a study that is almost

24
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
cruel in how simple it is. They gathered people who were in committed relationships and

25
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
asked them to picture the worst.

26
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
For half of them, the scenario was their own partner being unfaithful.

27
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
For the other half, it was a close friend's partner being unfaithful.

28
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Same betrayal. Same questions afterward.

29
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
The only thing that moved was whose life the wound belonged to.

30
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Then they measured how wisely each person reasoned about the mess.

31
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Wisdom here has nothing to do with intelligence or a test score.

32
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,000
It means a specific set of moves your mind can either make or fail to make.

33
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Admitting the limits of what you actually know.

34
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Seeing the situation from more than one side.

35
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
Expecting things to shift instead of assuming this feeling is permanent.

36
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Hunting for a compromise instead of needing to win.

37
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
The people reasoning about their friend's betrayal did all of it.

38
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
They were humble, balanced, open to being wrong.

39
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
The people reasoning about their own did almost none of it.

40
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Same brain, same room,

41
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:32,000
same hour, and the wisdom drained out the instant the problem had their name on it.

42
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Why would that happen? The answer is a quiet force called psychological distance.

43
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,000
When a problem belongs to someone else, you watch it from the outside,

44
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,000
like a scene through a window. You can see the whole room,

45
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
the exits, the furniture,

46
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
the way out. When the problem is yours, you are not watching the scene at all.

47
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
You are standing inside it, soaked in every detail,

48
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
flooded with everything it seems to say about you.

49
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Kross calls that flooded state immersion.

50
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Immersion feels like taking your problem seriously.

51
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:07,000
It is actually the thing that stops you from solving it,

52
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
because you are pressed too close to the glass to

53
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
see the shape of the room you are trapped in. So Grossmann and Kross tried one more thing,

54
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
and this is the part that matters most.

55
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
They took people back into their own problem,

56
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,000
the same one that had just made them foolish, and asked them to think about it from a distance.

57
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,000
To picture it from across the room.

58
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
To watch it the way they would watch it happen to somebody else.

59
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
And the gap closed. The very people who had reasoned poorly

60
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,000
about their own lives began reasoning about them as wisely as

61
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
they had about their friends. Nothing changed but the vantage point.

62
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
The wisdom was never gone. It had been locked on the far side of a window the whole time,

63
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
and distance was the key that fit the lock.

64
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Ozlem Ayduk, who has spent years studying this trick with Kross,

65
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
found the same thing again and again: step back to the fly on the wall,

66
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,000
and the emotional flooding drops, the racing settles,

67
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
and the part of you that thinks clearly finally gets a word in.

68
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Here is the strange part, though. If you can reason this well about your own

69
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,000
life the second you step back from it, why did your mind ever

70
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
bolt the door in the first place? Why would you evolve to be brilliant for

71
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,000
other people and nearly useless for yourself?

72
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
To answer that you have to leave the laboratory and travel back about two hundred thousand years,

73
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,000
into the only living conditions your brain was ever actually shaped for.

74
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
For almost the entire human story

75
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
you did not live anything like this.

76
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,000
You lived in a small band, a camp of maybe thirty or forty people who

77
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,000
had known you since the day you were born.

78
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
And here is the thing modern life has quietly deleted from your world:

79
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,000
you were almost never alone with a hard decision.

80
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
The anthropologist Richard B. Lee spent years among the Ju/'hoansi,

81
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
hunter-gatherers of the Kalahari, and one of the things he documented is

82
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
how a camp actually makes up its mind.

83
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Nobody wandered off to decide the big things in private.

84
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Where to move next. How to divide a kill. How to cool a feud between two

85
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
families before it turned to blood. It got talked out, in the open,

86
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
sometimes for days, around the fire,

87
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:18,000
everybody leaning in with an opinion until the group drifted toward something like agreement.

88
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Sit with what that did to your brain. The hardest problems of a human life were processed out loud,

89
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
by many people, from the outside.

90
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
The distance you now have to manufacture with a psychology trick

91
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,000
used to be built into the very walls of the camp.

92
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
You never needed a private tool for reasoning wisely about your own dilemmas,

93
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
because your dilemmas were never only yours.

94
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,000
They belonged to the whole circle, and the circle saw them through a window,

95
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
because to everyone except you,

96
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:52,000
a window is exactly what they were looking through. And there was a second pressure leaning on you from the same direction.

97
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,000
In a band, the person who gave good counsel was worth their weight in water.

98
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
If you could calm a fight, or spot the smart move nobody else had seen,

99
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,000
people owed you, protected you, kept you close to the fire.

100
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
Being wise for others earned you a place in the group.

101
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Being wise for yourself earned you almost nothing, because the group already had your problems covered.

102
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,000
So the wiring that got rewarded, generation after generation after generation,

103
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
was the wiring aimed outward, at everyone else's trouble.

104
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,000
Natural selection is a miser. It does not build a second private tool for

105
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
a job the tribe already does for free,

106
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
and it does not sharpen a skill that nobody in the camp ever claps for.

107
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
Remember what the stakes actually were. A person cast out of the band did

108
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
not stride off to become gloriously independent.

109
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,000
They died. Alone on that landscape,

110
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
without the many eyes and many hands of the group,

111
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
you were simply food. So a brain that made you useful to others,

112
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:55,000
that made people want to keep you around, was a brain that lived long enough to pass itself on.

113
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:00,000
The outward-facing counselor inside you is not a charming personality quirk.

114
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:04,000
It is a survival machine, tuned across ten thousand generations for a

115
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:08,000
world in which your problems were always handled inside a ring of

116
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
people who could see you clearly. Now look at the life you are actually living.

117
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
The ring is gone. You make the largest decisions a human being can make,

118
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,000
the job, the move, the person,

119
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
the message your thumb is hovering over at midnight,

120
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,000
and you make almost all of them alone, inside your own skull,

121
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
in the one voice your brain trusts least for the task.

122
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:35,000
The counsel that used to arrive from thirty people around a fire now has to

123
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:40,000
come from a single mind, and that mind is the one perspective evolution

124
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
never bothered to equip for advising itself.

125
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,000
That is the mismatch in one sentence. You are not worse at your own

126
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
life than your ancestors were at theirs.

127
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:53,000
You are attempting something they almost never had to do.

128
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,000
Facing your hardest choices with no council left in the room.

129
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
That is why the friend on the phone gets the very best of your thinking

130
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:04,000
and you are left with the worst of it. It was never hypocrisy.

131
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,000
It is a missing chair. The camp that used to sit in a circle around

132
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:11,000
your problem has shrunk down to one exhausted person, and that person is standing far too close to the

133
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
fire to make out the shape of anything.

134
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
Which is exactly why the repair is so small, and exactly why it works.

135
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:23,000
You cannot summon the whole band back into your kitchen at one in the morning.

136
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,000
But you can rebuild the single thing the band actually gave you,

137
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
the view from outside, and you can do it with nothing more

138
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:32,000
than a change in the words you use.

139
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
Grossmann and Kross found that the entire vantage point can

140
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,000
flip on something as tiny as a pronoun.

141
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:42,000
When you turn your problem over as an I, you drop straight back inside it

142
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,000
and the flooding starts. When you address yourself by name instead,

143
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
when you frame the question the way a friend across the table would frame it,

144
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,000
you take one clean step back to the window.

145
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:57,000
So the next time you are stuck, do not ask what you should do.

146
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Ask what you would tell a friend standing in this exact situation.

147
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
Better still, put your own name in the sentence.

148
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,000
Not why can I never figure this out, but a plain question about

149
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,000
what this particular person, the one with your name, ought to do next.

150
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
It sounds far too silly to change anything. It is one of the most reliably tested

151
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,000
findings in this entire corner of psychology.

152
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
The moment your problem is spoken in the third person,

153
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
the wise reasoning you hand out to everyone else for free comes quietly back online,

154
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,000
and points, for once, at you.

155
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,000
You are not learning a new skill tonight. You already own it.

156
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
You prove you own it every single time somebody you love calls you

157
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:38,000
in the dark and leaves the call steadier than they arrived.

158
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
You are only learning to stand where you have always been able to stand for other people,

159
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:46,000
and then turn, and look back at your own life through the same clear glass.

160
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
So the next time it is you,

161
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
late at night, wrecked over a decision,

162
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,000
do not wait for the phone to ring. Be the friend on the other end of the line.

163
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:01,000
Ask yourself what you would tell them, say your own name out loud, and then listen to the answer you already knew.

164
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:05,000
You always knew what other people should do. It turns out one of those people was you.
